<title>AUSTRALIA: RTRS-AUSTRALIA EMPLOYMENT ROSE 21,300 IN JULY.</title>
<headline>RTRS-AUSTRALIA EMPLOYMENT ROSE 21,300 IN JULY.</headline>
<dateline>SYDNEY 1997-08-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>Australia's seasonally adjusted estimate of the number of employed persons rose 21,300 in July to 8.414 million, against 8.392 million in June and 8.342 million a year earlier, the Bureau of Statistics said.</p>
<p>The seasonally adjusted unemployment rate rose to 8.7 percent from 8.5 percent in June, compared with 8.6 percent in July 1996. Full-time employment rose around 52,500, while part-time fell 31,200.</p>
<p>Economists had on average expected employment to rise around 25,000 in July, with the unemployment rate firming to 8.6 percent.  </p>
<p>AUSTRALIAN UNEMPLOYMENT</p>
<p>    (Seasonally adj)     July  97    June  97     July  96</p>
<p>    Unemployment Rate     8.7 pct     8.5 pct	8.6 pct</p>
<p>    Employed		 8.41 million    8.39 million     8.34 million</p>
<p>    Unemployed		801,200     777,800	780,500</p>
<p>    Participation Rate   63.2 pct    63.0 pct     63.5 pct</p>
<p>(Unadjusted)</p>
<p>    Unemployment Rate     8.2 pct     8.2 pct	8.0 pct</p>
<p>    Employed		 8.45 million    8.42 million     8.38 million</p>
<p>    Unemployed		750,900     750,800	731,600</p>
<p>    Participation Rate   63.1 pct    63.0 pct     63.4 pct</p>
<p>(Trend Estimates)</p>
<p>    Unemployment Rate    8.7 pct	8.7 pct	8.6 pct</p>
<p>    Employed		8.40 million     8.40 million     8.35 million</p>
<p>    Unemployed	     795,600	797,700	785,600</p>
<p>    Participation Rate  63.1 pct     63.2 pct     63.6 pct</p>
<p>The Bureau said the seasonally adjusted unemployment rate rose mainly due to a rise of 31,800 males looking for full-time work.  </p>
<p>Seasonally adjusted unemployment rates by states:</p>
<p>			     July 1997   June 97    July  1996</p>
<p>    New South Wales     8.4 pct     7.6 pct     7.9 pct</p>
<p>    Victoria		8.9 pct     9.4 pct     8.7 pct</p>
<p>    Queensland	    9.1 pct     8.9 pct     9.2 pct</p>
<p>    South Australia     9.8 pct     9.7 pct     9.3 pct</p>
<p>    Western Australia   7.2 pct     7.2 pct     7.6 pct</p>
<p>    Tasmania	     11.2 pct    11.0 pct    11.1 pct</p>
<p>-- Sydney Newsroom 61-2 9373-1800</p>
